      "text": "$\\textbf{{A}}\\ > 9 \\qquad \\textbf{{B}}\\ 10 \\qquad \\textbf{{C}}\\ 11 \\qquad \\textbf{{D}}\\ 12 \\qquad \\textbf{{E}}\\ 13$ To solve the problem of finding the length of the longest side of the similar triangle, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the scale factor:**\n   The perimeter of the original triangle is \\( P = 6 + 7 + 9 = 22 \\) cm.\n   The perimeter of the similar triangle is given as 110 cm.\n   The scale factor \\( k \\) is the ratio of the perimeters:\n   \\[\n   k = \\frac{110}{22} = 5\n   \\]\n   This means that each side of the similar triangle is 5 times longer than the corresponding side of the original triangle.\n\n2. **Find the sides of the similar triangle:**\n   Each side of the original triangle is 2 cm (since \\( 6 \\div 5 = 1.2 \\), \\( 7 \\div 5 = 1.4 \\), and \\( 9 \\div 5 = 1.8 \\)). \n   Therefore, the corresponding sides of the similar triangle are:\n   \\[\n   1.2 \\times 5 = 6 \\, \\text{cm}, \\quad 1.4 \\times 5 = 7 \\, \\text{cm}, \\quad 1.8 \\times 5 = 9 \\, \\text{cm}\n   \\]\n   The longest side of the similar triangle is:\n   \\[\n   9 \\times 5 = 45 \\, \\text{cm}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Verify the longest side of the original triangle:**\n   The longest side of the original triangle is \\( 9 \\, \\text{cm} \\), which is \\( 1.8 \\times 5 = 9 \\, \\text{cm} \\).\n\nThus, the longest side of the similar triangle is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{13 \\, \\text{cm}}\n\\]",
